o><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Tb</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>65</mn><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>Tm</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>69</mn></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "043395", "text": "$\\mathrm{Sm}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286da6"}, {"id": "8dcd4f", "text": "$\\mathrm{Yb}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286da7"}, {"id": "d4343a", "text": "$\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Tb}^{4+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286da8"}, {"id": "a2c693", "text": "$\\mathrm{Tb}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Tm}^{4+}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286da9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286daa", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["6162fb5a6ed13040ec84de88"], "concepts": ["6162fb796ed13040ec84f520"], "question": {"text": "Amongst the following statements, that which was not proposed by Dalton was :"}, "options": [{"id": "1a7279", "text": "chemical reactions involve reorganization of atoms. These are neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dab"}, {"id": "fa85af", "text": "all the atoms of a given element have identical properties including identical mass. Atoms of different elements differ in mass.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dac"}, {"id": "e59fc3", "text": "when gases combine or reproduced in a chemical reaction they do so in a simple ratio by volume provided all gases are at the same T &amp; P.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dad"}, {"id": "b48dbc", "text": "matter consists of indivisible atoms.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dae"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286daf", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87f1"], "question": {"text": "Correct statements for an element with atomic number 9 are<br />A. There can be 5 electrons for which $\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=+\\frac{1}{2}$ and 4 electrons for which $\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{s}}=-\\frac{1}{2}$<br />B. There is only one electron in $p_z$ orbital<br />C. The last electron goes to orbital with $\\mathrm{n}=2$ and $l=1$<br />4. The sum of angular nodes of all the atomic orbitals is 1.<br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below:"}, "options": [{"id": "9d7207", "text": "C and D Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db0"}, {"id": "7dbb8b", "text": "A and C Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db1"}, {"id": "54723d", "text": "A, C and D Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db2"}, {"id": "cb2407", "text": "A and B Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["6162fb796ed13040ec84f51d"], "question": {"text": "Match List I with List II\n\\(\\begin{array}{|l|l|l|l|}\n\\hline & \\text{ List - I (Element) } & & \\text{List - II (Electronic configuration)} \\\\\n\\hline \\text { A. } & \\mathrm{N} & \\text { I. } & {[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^2 4 \\mathrm{p}^5 \\mathrm{AR}} \\\\\n\\hline \\text { B. } & \\mathrm{S} & \\text { II. } & {[\\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \\mathrm{~s}^2 3 \\mathrm{p}^4} \\\\\n\\hline \\text { C. } & \\mathrm{Br} & \\text { III. } & {[\\mathrm{He}] 2 \\mathrm{~s}^2 2 \\mathrm{p}^3} \\\\\n\\hline \\text { D. } & \\mathrm{Kr} & \\text { IV. } & {[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^2 4 \\mathrm{p}^6} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\\)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:"}, "options": [{"id": "d39bc1", "text": "A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db5"}, {"id": "47b0b1", "text": "A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db6"}, {"id": "fe3cf7", "text": "A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db7"}, {"id": "385dba", "text": "A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286db9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b471c"], "question": {"text": "<p>The energy of an electron in the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math> Its energy in the third Bohr orbit is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "b14bf4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mi>th</mi></math>&nbsp;of the value", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dba"}, {"id": "9dd87b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;of this value", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dbb"}, {"id": "0aa921", "text": "Three times of this value", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dbc"}, {"id": "d2c4b3", "text": "One third of this value", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dbd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dbe", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5671"], "question": {"text": "The de Broglie wavelength $(\\lambda)$ associated with a photoelectron varies with the frequency $(v)$ of the incident radiation as,$\\left[v_{0}\\right.$ is threshold frequency]:"}, "options": [{"id": "295308", "text": "$\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dbf"}, {"id": "1ed634", "text": "$\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{4}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc0"}, {"id": "ccda75", "text": "$\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc1"}, {"id": "cc7f6f", "text": "$\\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477c"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52c0"], "question": {"text": "Heat treatment of muscular pain involves radiation of wavelength of about 900 nm . Which spectral line of H atom is suitable for this?\nGiven : Rydberg constant $\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}=10^5 \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}, \\mathrm{~h}=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~s}, \\mathrm{c}=3 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$ )"}, "options": [{"id": "006347", "text": "Balmer series, $\\infty \\rightarrow 2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc4"}, {"id": "1a4050", "text": "Lyman series, $\\infty \\rightarrow 1$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc5"}, {"id": "0d5799", "text": "Paschen series, $\\infty \\rightarrow 3$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc6"}, {"id": "de9af2", "text": "Paschen series, $5 \\rightarrow 3$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f722ee0e063d9286dc7"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "202